      "id": "lem-pointwise-cdf-convergence-to-a-continuous-law-is-uniform",
      "concern": "Step 1.1 asserts: “The supremum sup_x|G_n(x)−F(x)| is measurable: right-continuity makes it equal to the supremum over rational x.” Paths are required to be CDFs only almost surely, and completeness is not assumed. Counterexample: use Borel [0,1] with restricted Lebesgue probability, a Borel null Cantor set N, and a non-Borel subset A⊂N. Let F be the continuous uniform [2,3] CDF and, for every n, set G_n(x)(ω)=F(x)+1{ω∈A and x=ω}. Every fixed-x evaluation is measurable and equals F(x) almost surely; outside N every path is exactly F. Nevertheless sup_x|G_n(x)−F(x)|=1_A is not measurable. Thus the asserted convergence in probability is not defined as written. An everywhere-CDF version convention or an appropriate completeness hypothesis is missing. The actual finite resampling CDFs used downstream have everywhere-CDF paths.",
      "severity": "would-be-fatal"
    },
    {
      "id": "lem-uniform-cdf-order-and-quantile-coverage",
      "concern": "Part 3 permits only almost surely CDF paths, but step 3.1 claims q_Ĝ(u) is measurable using the rational quantile identity, which need not hold on the exceptional set. On Borel [0,1] with restricted Lebesgue probability, take a null Cantor set N and a non-Borel A⊂N. Let X≡3/2, F be its CDF, and Ĝ(x)(ω)=F(x)+1{ω∈A and x=ω}. Each evaluation is measurable and paths equal F outside N. With ε=2 and δ=0 the stated uniform-error event is the whole space, so its probability hypothesis holds. But q_Ĝ(1/2)=ω on A and 3/2 otherwise. For α₁=0, α₂=1/2, the coverage event is Ω\\A, which is not measurable. Therefore part 3 is not well-defined under its stated hypotheses. Specify versions on the exceptional set, require CDF paths everywhere, or impose suitable completeness.",
